 ## Overview
-Multiresolution Rendering with Data Reduction is a technique that exploits hierarchical data compression to achieve super-linear performance increases in volume ray casting by computing fewer voxels and increasing step lengths for low-resolution blocks [S1, unofficial]. It solves the computational bottleneck of large volumetric datasets by adaptively selecting resolution levels based on ray traversal, allowing for interactive frame rates on data that would otherwise be too large for real-time rendering [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard multiresolution methods that primarily use lower resolutions for distant objects or early termination, this method explicitly applies a data reduction ratio to volume blocks, trading visual fidelity for significant speedups in global illumination calculations [S1, unofficial].
+Multiresolution Rendering with Data Reduction is a rendering approach that exploits data reduction across resolution levels to achieve super-linear performance increases by computing fewer voxels and increasing step lengths for low-resolution blocks [S1, unofficial]. It solves the computational bottleneck of large volumetric datasets by adaptively selecting resolution levels based on ray traversal, allowing for interactive frame rates on data that would otherwise be too large for real-time rendering [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard multiresolution methods that primarily use lower resolutions for distant objects or early termination, this method explicitly applies a data reduction ratio to volume blocks, trading visual fidelity for significant speedups in global illumination calculations [S1, unofficial].
 
